UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037006100?origin=resultslist
AD  - Arab Open University, Faculty of Computer Studies, A'ali, Bahrain
AB  - Industry 4.0 has enabled the use of Internet of Things (IoT) sensing and automated data analytics, making Predictive Maintenance (PdM) essential for identifying early equipment faults. However, many PdM systems rely on centralized cloud processing, which raises privacy concerns and struggles with heterogeneous Non-Independent and Identically Distributed (Non-IID) industrial data. To address these limitations, this paper introduces a Hybrid Edge-Cloud Federated Learning (FL) framework that performs local training on edge devices while aggregating global updates in the cloud. The proposed method is evaluated using the Case Western Reserve University (CWRU) bearing dataset, a widely-adopted benchmark in industrial fault diagnosis research. Results show that the federated model achieves accuracy comparable to centralized learning while preserving data privacy and maintaining robustness under Independent and Identically Distributed (IID) and Non-IID conditions. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) explainability further highlights the most influential features for fault classification, supporting transparent deployment in industrial environments. © 2025 IEEE.
