AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping the field of medical imaging and diagnostics, evolving from experimental applications to an essential role in clinical practice. Through machine and deep learning approaches, AI systems now enhance image quality, homogenize acquisition protocols, accelerate interpretation, and support complex decision-making processes. For various imaging modalities (e.g., X-ray, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), ultrasound, positron emission tomography (PET), and digital pathology), AI-improved methods have made significant advancements in image reconstruction, noise reduction, anatomical segmentation, and quantitative measurement. These developments, in addition to advancing diagnostic accuracy, reduce interpretation time and thus facilitate more efficient and patient-centric care. In addition to image processing, computer-aided detection, decision-support systems, and predictive analytics are increasingly incorporating AI tools in diagnostic workflows. Multimodal imaging captures different kinds of information about tissue structures and functions, through the integration of imaging data, genomic data, laboratory results, and clinical history. This facilitates the implementation of precision medicine, which provides personalized treatment and enhanced prognostic ability. In particular, digital pathology has made a huge impact through the implementation of AI algorithms that can analyze gigapixel whole-slide images with speed and consistency that cannot be matched by established methodologies. Despite these achievements, the widespread incorporation of AI-driven clinical applications faces persistent challenges. Complications, linked with data integrity, algorithm validation, interpretability, ethical monitoring, and especially with privacy and equity, remain important barriers. New techniques, such as explainable AI, federated learning, and collaborative research designs, aim at addressing these challenges, thus facilitating transparency, fairness, and regulatory compliance. Successful integration of AI in diagnostic methodologies in medicine depends on finding a balance between technological innovation, professional responsibility, and ethical accountability. When diligently implemented, AI has the potential to provide diagnostics that are not only faster and more accurate but also safer, fairer, and profoundly aligned with patient-centered principles. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Chetna Sharma, Amandeep Kaur, Harpreet Singh individual chapters, the contributors.
