AB  - The increasing demand for intelligent, responsive, and privacy-preserving healthcare systems has led to the exploration of advanced computing and communication paradigms. This work presents CogniHealth 6G-X, a comprehensive, next-generation framework that integrates 6G-enabled ultra-reliable communication, federated learning, blockchain security, edge intelligence, and digital twin technology to deliver secure, interpretable, and real-time healthcare solutions. The proposed architecture is organized into five cohesive layers: Sensing and Data Acquisition, Edge Intelligence, Federated Learning, Blockchain-Based Security, and Digital Twin with Cloud Orchestration. Together, these layers ensure low-latency inference, decentralized learning without compromising data privacy, robust tamper-proof security, and context-aware simulation-driven decision support. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that the framework reduces end-to-end latency by over 80% compared to cloud-only models, achieves a prediction accuracy of 96.8%, and lowers bandwidth consumption by nearly 70%, making it well-suited for both urban and resource-limited settings. The federated models, which use real-time personalization and synthetic data created through digital twins, work better than traditional centralized systems while keeping patient data local. Also, the use of blockchain ensures that all actions are trackable, access is controlled, and transactions are secure, with a 99.4% chance of detecting any tampering. Another important part of the system is explainable AI, which helps build trust among doctors, as 91.6% of them accept the system’s decisions. This single system fixes many issues with current eHealth models and provides a solution that is scalable, secure, and practical for modern healthcare. The CogniHealth 6G-X system sets the stage for future health networks that are centered around patients and can deliver reliable care in many different settings and situations. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Upinder Kaur, Aparna Kumari, Hemant Kumar Saini, Surbhi B. Khan, and Mariya Ouaissa; individual chapters, the contributors.
