AD  - Chandigarh University, Mohali, India
AB  - The use of social media has led to more communication and connectivity, but it comes with many challenges. The existing tools are primarily reactive, content-based and unable to predict escalation trends based on the behavior of users; they are therefore not very good in safeguarding vulnerable users. This paper suggests a Hybrid AI and Behavioral Analytics Framework that integrates content-intelligence, which uses deep learning to recognize internet-based threats against women proactively, with the behavioral exposure modeling to forecast and mitigate such threats. The proposed framework takes advantage of gender-sensitive identification of abuse through language intelligence on transformers, examining exposure risks on a temporal basis through analysis of behavior, and future prediction of online harassment through predictive modeling. A risk score is turned into a composite risk score to dynamically develop adaptive protection measures like comment filtering, blocking interactions, and escalation notifications. Transparency and regulatory adherence are ensured through privacy-preserving tools, which include pseudonymous identifiers, encrypted feature analysis, and explainable AI outputs. The trials demonstrate that the results are more accurate in early detection, reduced false positives, and mitigated in a timely manner than conventional moderating tools. © 2026 IEEE.
