TI  - Differentially Private and Explainable Federated Learning for ICU Mortality Prediction
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2026 4th International Conference on Secure Cyber Computing and Communications, ICSCCC 2026
SP  - 892
EP  - 898
DO  - 10.1109/ICSCCC69031.2026.11600079
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046239214?origin=resultslist
AD  - Rk College Of Engineering(A), Dept. Of Computer Science And Engineering, Vijayawada, India
AB  - Accurate ICU mortality prediction is vital for timely care but is constrained by strict privacy rules and the need for interpretable models. This paper proposes DP-IG-FL, a federated learning framework that ensures data privacy while enabling transparent and collaborative prediction across multiple hospitals. The framework is built on an LSTM-based federated architecture, where Differentially Private Stochastic Gradient Descent (DP-SGD) is employed to prevent information leakage during model updates, and Integrated Gradients (IG) is used to provide feature-level explanations aligned with clinical reasoning. The proposed approach is evaluated using two widely adopted ICU benchmarks, MIMIC-III and eICU, under realistic multi-hospital settings. Experimental results show that DP-IG-FL achieves an AUROC of 0.89, outperforming standard FedAvg-LSTM by 5.3% and centralized LSTM models by 2.4%. Notably, the model retains more than 97% of its predictive performance even under a strict privacy budget of ϵ = 1.0, demonstrating strong resilience to privacy constraints. Explainability highlights key predictors like creatinine, heart rate, blood pressure, and oxygen levels, improving trust in the model. Overall, DP-IG-FL illustrates that high predictive accuracy, rigorous privacy guarantees, and clinically interpretable insights can be achieved simultaneously, supporting the development of trustworthy AI systems for critical-care decision support. © 2026 IEEE.
