AU  - Akçakale, A.H.
AU  - Uğural, M.N.
TI  - Interpretable Machine Learning for Cost Estimation in Public Restoration Projects: An Empirical Analysis of Turkish Cultural Heritage Data
PY  - 2026
T2  - Advances in Civil Engineering
VL  - 2026
IS  - 1
C7  - 9234704
DO  - 10.1155/adce/9234704
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053654075?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil Engineering, Istanbul Kultur University, Istanbul, Turkey
AB  - Globally, the primary barrier to adopting advanced analytics in public administration is often not a lack of data but the legal incompatibility of “black-box” predictive models with statutory requirements for transparency. This study addresses this governance challenge by developing an interpretable machine learning (ML) framework designed as an auditable solution for predicting pretender costs under strict regulatory environments, such as Turkish Public Procurement Laws No. 4734 and 5018. Utilizing a unique dataset of 578 projects from Türkiye’s Directorate General of Cultural Assets and Museums, this study suggests that inherently interpretable models, specifically classification and regression trees (CART) and chi-square automatic interaction detection (CHAID), outperform traditional linear regression. Validated through 10-fold cross-validation and a temporal holdout protocol, the CART model yielded a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) on a logarithmic scale of 8.200%, a root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.250, and a coefficient of determination (R2) of 0.663, identifying critical nonlinear risk thresholds, such as a four-fold cost escalation beyond a 100-day project duration. A key outcome was a preliminarily validated prototype decision-support tool that generated a “Decision Justification Report,” explicitly mapping project risks to auditable thresholds. This study provides a preliminarily validated, scalable framework for deploying AI in regulated public finance domains, with potential applicability to other similar regulatory contexts, helping to reconcile the tension between predictive power and institutional accountability. Copyright © 2026 Ali Haydar Akçakale and Mehmet Nurettin Uğural. Advances in Civil Engineering published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
