UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105054236160?origin=resultslist
AD  - Harz University of Applied Sciences, Faculty of Business Studies, Wernigerode, 38855, Germany
AB  - AI-based decision-support systems often achieve high predictive performance but remain difficult to use when their outputs are not explainable, monitored, or connected to human action. This paper develops a self-monitoring explainable AI decision-support architecture for pre-publication communication decisions, in which monitoring generates diagnostic signals while resulting actions remain under human control. The architecture combines five layers: pre-decision input construction, XGBoost-based prediction, SHAP-based explanation, residual monitoring, and a knowledge/action layer for human-in-the-loop use. It is evaluated on 10,728 pre-publication content decisions from 16 official public-sector Facebook channels using chronology-preserving validation with train-only preprocessing. Decision-utility analyses show that the model supports ordinal differentiation between lower- and higher-potential posts. The model primarily exploits the recent communication context of the channel, while structural and format-related characteristics provide additional decision-relevant information. Temporal stress testing and residual monitoring show why the system should not be deployed as a static prediction model, but as a monitored decision-support architecture. Thus, the paper contributes a design pattern for transparent, traceable, and human-overseen AI use in dynamic organisational environments, inspired by selected EU AI Act principles. © 2026 The Authors
