AD  - Faculty of Information Science and Technology, Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia (UKM), Selangor Darul Ehsan, Bangi, 43600, Malaysia
AD  - Faculty of Computer and Mathematical Science, Universiti Teknologi MARA (UiTM), Selangor Darul Ehsan, Shah Alam, 40450, Malaysia
AB  - Teacher-school matching in many systems remains manual, slow, and hard to justify, leading to subject mismatch and uneven distribution. This paper proposes an adaptive decision model that connects policy rules with algorithmic optimization to support transparent placement decisions. The work follows a Design Science Research (DSR) approach and draws on decision theory and multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM). Criteria and weights are elicited using Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), alternatives are scored with Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS), and feasible assignments are searched using a soft-computing genetic algorithm (GA) under real policy constraints (quotas, subject requirements, distance, hardship). We position the model against matching-market ideas from the assignment literature and argue that explainable outputs showing the top factors behind each recommendation are necessary to build trust with stakeholders and to support appeals or audits. A prototype support system operationalizes the model and is evaluated on simulated scenarios and state cases. Results indicate faster processing, lower teacher–subject mismatch, and strong agreement with expert judgements, with positive usability feedback from practitioners. The paper contributes (i) a clear framework linking criteria, constraints, and outcomes, (ii) a prescriptive, explainable model for public-sector placement, and (iii) evidence from an implementable prototype. The approach is practical, auditable, and adaptable to future policy updates.Across simulated and state cases, processing time decreased by [x]%, subject mismatch fell by [y] pp., and agreement with experts remained substantial (κ = [z]). © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
