AD  - Graphic Era Deemed to be University, Uttarakhand, Dehradun, India
AD  - School of Computer Science and Engineering, Galgotias University, Uttar Pradesh, Greater Noida, India
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) has shown great promise in aiding clinical decision-making in diagnostic imaging, predictive analytics, personalized medicine, and treatment planning. However, the great predictive power of many models in practice has a “black box” property, which limits their practical adoption for clinicians, regulators, and patients who demand transparency and accountability regarding life-or-death decisions. Explainable AI (XAI) has been proposed to fill the gap by offering reasons relevant to humans for model predictions, yet approaches that have been developed so far encounter significant issues related to robustness, clinical utility, and applicability in healthcare workflows. This chapter then discusses the state-of-the-art of XAI in clinical deployment, and points out some future research opportunities which could help assure a safe and accurate introduction of AI in medicine. It covers the limitations of state-of-the-art explanation techniques, along with several aspects like fidelity, stability, bias propagation, and discrepancy with clinicians’ reasoning. Next, it describes the significance of human-centered design that assumes explanation methods should be adjusted to various clinical contexts from time-pressured emergency care situations to prospective monitoring over patients. It also addresses the importance of strong evaluation frameworks that go beyond technical measures, but incorporate human factors, trust calibration, and patient outcomes. Regulatory and ethical considerations are combined, focusing on how explainability interacts with safety, responsibility, and fairness in healthcare. Lastly, it provides future perspectives for an XAI research agenda around bias-aware and causal explanations, interactive systems, and clinician-in-the-loop approaches, the role of prospective clinical trials in XAI, as well as standardized audit mechanisms for regulatory compliance. By connecting technical innovation to clinical utility, ethical oversight, and translational research, XAI can step out of the realm of theoretical potential into that of a realistic path for radical improvements in patient care. The purpose of this chapter is to provide a roadmap for researchers, clinicians, and policy makers wishing to navigate along the ramp from developmental tools to reliable clinical collaborators in XAI. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
