AD  - Srm Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Computing Technologies, Kattankulathur, Chennai, India
AD  - Zs Associates, Bengaluru, India
AB  - The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) allows incessant observation of patients in smart hospital wards by the use of connected sensors and wearable devices. Nonetheless, there are several issues of low interpretability, poor reliability, and scalable implementation of many current systems of IoMT analytics. The proposed IoMT-HealNet is an end-to-end IoMT analytics solution that aims at predicting the recovery time of patients in smart ward settings. Its architecture incorporates pre-processing structured data, privacy preserving feature engineering, stacked ensemble learning architecture based on LightGBM and Extra Trees base learners with ElasticNet as the meta-learner. Bayesian optimization is implemented to enhance the use of hyperparameters and predictive qualities. To increase the level of transparency, explainable AI methods such as LIME and SHAP are introduced to offer local and global explanation of model predictions. Moreover, the framework has an MLOps layer tracking of experiments, reproducibility, and drift monitoring with MLflow as well as an AgentOps layer that allows automated decision support and human-in-the-loop validation. The experimental assessment shows a good predictive ability of R2 at 0.93, RMSE of 0.93 and MAE of 0.70, which shows a high practical applicability to a clinical setting. Use of explainability and operational governance of the IoMT-HealNet facilitates reliable AI-aided decision making and can be implemented in the future in intelligent healthcare monitoring systems.  © 2026 IEEE.
