AB  - The quick expansion of IoT-related medical technologies and AI-based diagnostics has boosted the creation of smart healthcare systems, and even with centralized learning, sensitive patient data remains vulnerable to privacy concerns, lack of transparency, and adversarial risks. In order to address these issues, this paper will suggest a Causal Blockchain-Governed Explainable Federated learning (CBGEFL) framework of trust-calibrated and privacy-preserving healthcare intelligence. The framework facilitates decentralized collaborative training among hospitals and wearable IoMT devices with federated learning without exchange of raw patient data. To obtain cause-effect explanations in terms of SHAP-based attribution, a CNN-LSTM model is combined with Structural Causal Model (SCM) to provide clear clinical decision support. The secure aggregation is governed by a lightweight Ethereum-based blockchain layer, which offers immutable logging, gradient integrity verification, and mitigation of poisoning attacks. Moreover, it has a dynamic trust-calibration scheme that calculates the reliability of nodes based on the consistency of accuracy, causal stability, and update divergence to perform weighted aggregation. Python, PyTorch, Flower, SHAP, and Ganache blockchain simulation are used to implement the system. Experimental testing on wearable IoMT and heart disease data shows better predictive accuracy, 96.1% accuracy and 95.4% F1-score, compared to the standard federated learning, which is about 4.9x greater. Security analysis indicates attack detection rate of 95.8% and trust index of 96.4, which is an improvement of more than 17% compared to the baseline models. Moreover, it minimizes nearly 29% and 25% communication overhead and energy consumption, respectively, and increases scalability with resource-constrained devices. Combining causal explainability and blockchain validation improves the area of adversarial robustness and retains efficiency. All in all, CBGEFL framework develops scalable, clear, and regulation-friendly infrastructures of next-generation trustful smart healthcare ecosystems. © 2026 IEEE.
