AD  - School of Computer Science Engineering and Technology, Bennett University, Uttar Pradesh, Greater Noida, India
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Graphic Era Deemed To Be University, Uttarakhand, Dehradun, India
AD  - School of Computing and Innovative Technologies, British University Vietnam, Hanoi, Viet Nam
AD  - Amity School of Engineering and Technology, Bihar, Patna, India
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming healthcare and becoming an enabler for optimization of diagnostics, treatment planning, as well as operational management. In this chapter, we explore the coming intersection of data—and diagnostic—with AI, an intersection through which we believe AI can transform the delivery of healthcare. According to the same algorithms, AI enables greater proactive and precise operation of the healthcare system, allowing them to detect the disease at its early stages, create a personalized treatment plan, and improve the patient’s life. By incorporating wearable technologies and Internet of Things devices, continuous patient monitoring is also made possible and is put into real-time interventions and is also to lessen the hospital dependency. In this chapter, we examine how AI is advancing drug discovery and clinical trials, faster analysis of huge datasets enabling drugs to be developed much more quickly, and with greater chances of success. The potential use of federated learning models as a potential solution to handle the data privacy challenges while allowing collaborative work between institutions, while abiding by privacy regulations is highlighted. Holistic and precise diagnostics are being paved by advanced AI-driven decision support systems that can interpret complex multimodal data. Operational efficiencies are another space AI helps, optimizing resource allocation, predicting patient inflow, and automating several routine administrative tasks. It explores what integrating quantum computing into AI would mean and how that could be used to answer difficult healthcare problems like protein folding and complex data analysis. Moreover, ethical concerns and the need for explainable AI systems are identified, to increase confidence in the trustworthiness and reliability in clinical settings. In this chapter, we offer an in-depth look at the progressive roll out of AI in healthcare, from its status today and into the future for a resilient, intelligent, and equitable healthcare ecosystem. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
