TI  - Algorithmic Bias in AI-Mediated Communication Systems: Transparency, Power Asymmetries, and the Role of Explainable Artificial Intelligence
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Smart Multidomain Integrated Learning Environments, ICSMILE 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICSMILE69273.2026.11519239
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041560409?origin=resultslist
AD  - Florida, United States
AD  - Virginia, United States
AB  - Artificial intelligence is permeating more and more our everyday communication, impacting the way our messages are filtered, prioritized, and evaluated. That happens not only through email providers or social networks but even to a great extent through automatic decision support systems. AI-based services, which are primarily intended to boost productivity and handle vast amounts of data, may however, inadvertently generate inequalities in reacting to different messages, users, and content thereby systematically discriminating some of them. Such forms of discrimination typically originate from training data imperfections, model biases, or lack of contextual transparency and may especially concern marginalized individuals and communities exhibiting peculiar linguistic or cultural traits. This research first of all identifies the different ways in which algorithmic discrimination can become visible in AI-supported communication. It also considers what this implies in terms of transparency and accountability for a communication system running on AI. To that end, the paper outlines an explainability-driven approach that brings together feature-level interpretation of models and human oversight with an accountability and responsibility framework. The proposed method makes it possible to have a more detailed understanding of automated utterances, making it easier to spot bias and to carry out well-informed interventions when the system is in operation. By embedding explanation and human discernment in the framework and management of communication systems, this paper moves toward a more responsible, unbiased, and equitable AI-enabled communication environment. © 2026 IEEE.
