AB  - The financial ecosystem is turning from decision-support systems to autonomous computerised systems capable of self-directing the optimization of their own performance in rapidly changing markets. This paper studies the use of agentic A.I. in core banking and capital market operations involving algorithmic execution, risk hedging, fraud detection, and regulatory oversight. This investigation studies learning systems for implementation, working systems for human-free reactivity and their implementation and functioning through multi-agent collaboration architecture, and transformer-based language models. Noteworthy methodological approaches employ deep reinforcement learning to develop high-frequency trading strategies, graph neural networks to detect anomalies, federated learning for privacy-preserving fraud detection, and transfer learning for domain-specific language model fine-tuning. Findings from industry adoption patterns show that autonomous agents are more accurate market forecasters and more efficient in their operations, but pose new challenges for model interpretability, algorithmic fairness and systemic risk. The most important hindrances to deployment are data quality, latency, and regulatory issues. A technological roadmap is proposed in the framework, which combines explainable Artificial Intelligence techniques, adversarial robustness mechanisms and neural-symbolic reasoning methods to guarantee transparent and compliance autonomous operations. This study provides financial institutions with a comprehensive reference architecture for the governing agentic AI while remaining free from any This study provides financial institutions with a reference architecture for governing agentic AI in regulated environments.  © 2026 IEEE.
