TI  - From Automation to Accountability: Security Architectures and Non-Human Identity Governance in Generative AI-Driven Systems Integration
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Symposium on Computer Applications and Industrial Electronics, ISCAIE
IS  - 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ISCAIE68866.2026.11576316
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044413326?origin=resultslist
AD  - Phoenix, United States
AD  - Seattle, United States
AB  - In today's world, the functional excellence of the industries relies on effective system integration. This review study focuses on the exploration of different methodologies of Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) to improve the integration process in various applications. This study reviews the existing state of the art on Gen AI methodologies for advanced capabilities like data mapping, workflow generation, knowledge representation, and real-time decision support. This review also examines the studies that highlight the testified benefits of GenAI, such as better integration speed, effective data mapping, better user satisfaction, and scalability. Moreover, the study also identifies the challenges in the form of data privacy, legacy system compatibility, and algorithmic explainability. This review article also takes a closer look at the security aspect, because the deployment of GenAI incurs unique attacks like training data poisoning, prompt injection, and non-human identity vulnerabilities. This work highlights the increasing need for solutions like zero-trust architecture, stronger identity management, and defense methods against adversarial strategies designed specifically according to the needs of GenAI systems. The study analyzes different use cases and other peer-reviewed studies to distinguish critical research gaps in the form of limited formal method verification for AI-based system integration, a lack of standard metrics for evaluation, and the issues of handling non-human identities. Considering the findings, the review then discusses possible recommendations, including phased deployment, stakeholder engagement, data governance, and the incorporation of explainable AI frameworks. The focus of this study is to offer insights into where the current state of the art stands and what the realistic direction for the future is. By concentrating on the security, scalability, and the responsible use, future work can build on the provided insights for effective system integration of GenAI.  © 2026 IEEE.
