TI  - Explainable AI and Large Language Model in Mental Health Support System
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Intelligent Technologies, CONIT 2026
DO  - 10.1109/CONIT69683.2026.11621498
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047336871?origin=resultslist
AD  - Galgotias University, School of Computing Science & Engineering, Greater Noida, India
AB  - With recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) and natural language processing (NLP), digital mental health support systems have emerged as promising tools to improve access to psychological care. Large language models (LLMs) have significantly enhanced conversational quality, contextual reasoning, and personalization in AI-assisted mental health counseling systems (MHCS). However, the black-box nature of LLMs raises critical concerns related to explainability, ethical accountability, user trust, and clinical reliability, particularly in sensitive mental healthcare applications. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis and evaluation of explainable AI (XAI) integrated LLM-based mental health support systems. A modular architecture is proposed that explicitly incorporates emotion analysis, explainability mechanisms, ethical guardrails, and human-in-the-loop oversight into the LLM reasoning pipeline. The system is evaluated against traditional machine learning-based and pure LLM-based approaches using multiple criteria, including response quality, explainability, ethical safety, and escalation accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed XAI-integrated MHCS significantly improves interpretability and user trust while reducing harmful and biased responses, without compromising conversational effectiveness. The study further discusses ethical, legal, and social implications associated with deploying LLMs in mental healthcare and identifies key challenges related to transparency, bias, and responsible deployment. By emphasizing explainability and ethical alignment as core design principles, this work contributes toward the development of trustworthy, clinically viable, and responsible AI-driven mental health support systems. © 2026 IEEE.
