TI  - Explainable Federated Learning Approach for Early Detection and Diagnosis of Brain Stroke in Clinical Healthcare Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 3rd International Conference on Integrated Intelligence and Communication Systems, ICIICS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICIICS67880.2026.11483396
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042703825?origin=resultslist
AD  - SR University, Department of CS&AI, Warangal, India
AD  - SR University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Warangal, India
AB  - Stroke continues to be among the major causes of death and disability globally. On the other hand, stroke predictive models developed using centralized machine learning approaches remain hampered by poor interpretability, issues related to data privacy, and inefficiency in processing decentralized patient data. In this study, to mitigate these challenges associated with stroke predictive tasks, there is a proposal for the development of an Explainable Federated Learning platform incorporated with a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network to develop a transparent stroke predictive model. Based on the Stroke Prediction Dataset available on Kaggle, the new model was tested among decentralized nodes to maintain data privacy. The new platform exhibited improved communication complexity and quicker model convergence compared to previous centralized approaches. The developed model indicated 98.9% accuracy, 98.6 % precision, 98.2 % recall rate, 99.1 % specificity rate, and 98.4% F1 score. Fundamentally, the Explainable Federated LSTM model offers a good enhancement in predictive accuracy, privacy maintenance, and interpretable results to help clinicians arrive at reliable and transparent decisions for early detection of stroke. This study focuses on the evaluation of an explainable federated learning system for tabular stroke prediction and is not oriented toward the development of new algorithms. The results are indicative that privacy-preserving training coupled with explainability will allow clinicians to make transparent and reliable decisions. © 2026 IEEE.
