AU  - Florea, A.M.
TI  - Explainability in AI-Driven Medical Diagnostics
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - 27th International Symposium on Symbolic and Numeric Algorithms for Scientific Computing, SYNASC 2025
SP  - 3
EP  - 9
DO  - 10.1109/SYNASC69064.2025.00007
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038344624?origin=resultslist
AD  - National University Of Science And Technology Politehnica Bucharest, Romania
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) and, in particular, deep learning have transformed medical image analysis and computer-aided diagnosis. State-of-the-art convolutional and transformer-based architectures now achieve or surpass human-level performance in tasks such as tumor detection, organ segmentation, or disease classification in radiology and histopathology. Yet these systems are typically realized as 'black boxes', whose internal representations and decision pathways are opaque to clinicians. In safety-critical, high-stakes environments such as healthcare, this lack of transparency undermines trust, complicates regulatory approval, and poses ethical risks related to bias, accountability, and patient autonomy. This paper presents a structured overview of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) for AI-driven medical diagnostics. We first clarify the concepts of interpretability and explainability and motivate their importance for clinical decision support. We then present a simplified taxonomy of XAI methods along four axes: relation to the model architecture, scope of explanation, timing of application, and relation to the underlying model. Within this framework, we review three major families of approaches that are already being explored in medical imaging: visualization-based methods, model-distillation and surrogate modeling, and intrinsically interpretable models. Throughout the paper, we discuss representative medical applications to illustrate how XAI methods can highlight clinically meaningful regions or concepts. We conclude by highlighting the role of XAI for regulatory compliance, ethical responsibility, and human-in-the-loop governance, and briefly outline future directions of research in XAI systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
