UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038085898?origin=resultslist
AD  - Chandigarh University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Mohali, India
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is now part of the current healthcare systems, especially clinical decision support systems as well as diagnostic imaging. Despite the high diagnostic accuracy of deep learning models, their practical implementation is still restricted because of issues that are connected to interpretability, algorithmic bias, data privacy, and accountability. The paper explores how the principles of ethically oriented AI, i.e., explainability, fairness, and privacy can be applied to the AI-based clinical decision-making systems. Explainable AI methods are also used to increase the transparency of the model and allow clinicians to understand the diagnostic predictions with visual and featurebased explanations. Learning strategies that are fairnessconscious are integrated to prevent demographic bias and achieve equal diagnostic performance between different groups of patients. Besides that, a privacy preserving federated learning is also examined to allow collaborative model training without a central storage of information, enabling the preservation of sensitive patient data. The results of experimental assessment based on publicly available medical imaging datasets indicate that ethically enhanced AI models are competitive in the diagnostic accuracy, and they are also expected to significantly enhance their interpretability, fairness, and data security. The results indicate that to foster trustful, open, and clinically credible decision support systems within health facilities, the ethical concerns of AI systems need to be directly incorporated into the design of such systems. © 2026 IEEE.
