AD  - Prathyusha Engineering College, Department of CSE, Chennai, India
AB  - Transparency and accountability in orphanage support systems remain significant challenges due to fragmented reporting structures and limited traceability of resource allocation decisions. The objective of this study is to design and evaluate a blockchain-integrated explainable artificial intelligence framework that enables transparent, data-driven resource allocation and longitudinal impact tracking in child welfare ecosystems. The proposed framework combines machine learning-based accountability classification and impact prediction with a permissioned blockchain architecture that records allocation decisions, explanations, and audit metadata in an immutable ledger. An XGBoost-based allocation engine is employed to model nonlinear relationships among financial efficiency, governance, and transparency indicators, while explainability techniques provide interpretable decision-level insights. Experimental evaluation using the Charity Navigator Scores & Expenses dataset demonstrates that the proposed model achieves an accountability classification AUC of 0.865 and reduces RMSE in impact prediction by approximately 38% compared to linear baseline models. Statistical significance testing confirms that these improvements are robust (p < 0.05). The integration of blockchain ensures tamper-resistant documentation and auditability without exposing sensitive data. The findings indicate that combining explainable AI with permissioned blockchain infrastructure substantially enhances decision transparency, predictive reliability, and governance trust in orphanage support systems. © 2026 IEEE.
