AD  - Institut Teknologi Sepuluh Nopember, Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Surabaya, Indonesia
AD  - Universitas Internasional Semen Indonesia, Department of Informatics, Gresik, 61122, Indonesia
AB  - The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into competency-based learning (CBL) is rapidly advancing the shift from knowledge memorization to mastery of measurable skills, with significant implications for both academic and organizational contexts. This study presents a systematic literature review of 34 peer-reviewed articles identified through a PRISMA-guided screening process in the Scopus database (2015-2025). Methodological quality was appraised using the APPRAISE-AI instrument across six evaluation domains, yielding a mean composite score of 9.82 out of 12 (median = 10.75), with 23 studies (67.6 %) classified as high quality, confirming the robustness and reliability of the evidence. Synthesizing insights on core AI capabilities, including adaptive systems, learning analytics, skill detection, explainable AI (XAI), and hybrid approaches, and their relationship with competency rubrics as the central mechanism in CBL, this study makes three contributions to the literature. First, competency rubrics are positioned as boundary objects that interface AIdriven assessment evidence with personalized and adaptive learning paths. Second, a Decision Support System (DSS) is embedded as a facilitation and governance layer, involving managers, subject matter experts, and Human Resources/Learning and Development (HR/L&D) units to ensure fairness, ethical accountability, and contextual relevance in organizational learning ecosystems. Third, a systematic translation mechanism converts technical AI metrics, including accuracy, Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and F1-score, into actionable organizational Key Performance Indicators (KPIs), encompassing time-to-proficiency, compliance index, error reduction, and learning adoption. This dual conceptual and practical contribution provides a clear roadmap for leveraging AI to support transparent, ethical, and effective workforce development in competency-based corporate learning settings. © 2026 IEEE.
