TI  - AI-enhanced blockchain technology for food traceability and sustainability
PY  - 2026
T2  - Sustainable Food Technology
DO  - 10.1039/d6fb00267f
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105054055183?origin=resultslist
AD  - Istanbul Technical University, Faculty of Chemical-Metallurgical Engineering, Department of Food Engineering, Maslak, Istanbul, Sarıyer, 34469, Turkey
AB  - Global food supply chains (FSCs) have become increasingly complex, creating growing demands for reliable traceability, food safety, fraud prevention, and sustainability assessment. Blockchain technology provides a decentralized and immutable infrastructure for recording supply chain events, whereas artificial intelligence (AI) enables advanced analytics for prediction, monitoring, and decision-making. This narrative review examines recent developments in blockchain-enabled food traceability and discusses how the integration of AI, blockchain, and Internet of Things (IoT) sensing technologies can transform conventional traceability systems into intelligent decision-support platforms. Evidence from the reviewed studies indicates that AI-enhanced blockchain systems are being applied across a broad range of food supply chain activities, including cold-chain monitoring, fraud detection, quality assessment, demand forecasting, food waste reduction, and sustainability assessment. Recent implementations increasingly integrate blockchain with smart contracts, cloud and edge computing, mobile applications, and IoT sensing technologies, enabling continuous data collection and near real-time analysis throughout the supply chain. Despite these advances, challenges related to data interoperability, scalability, reliable data acquisition, AI model validation, and governance continue to limit wider adoption. To address these challenges, researchers have explored standardized data models, explainable AI, federated learning, and the recording of AI model metadata on blockchain to improve transparency and auditability. Overall, blockchain provides a trusted foundation for data integrity and traceability, while AI extends its value by enabling predictive analytics and data-driven decision-making. Future progress will depend on interoperable system architectures, high-quality data, and robust AI implementation to achieve measurable improvements in food safety, operational efficiency, and sustainability. This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry, 2026.
