TI  - Beyond Collected Data: Surfacing and Controlling Shadow Inferences in Everyday Workflows
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
VL  - 16730 LNCS
SP  - 24
EP  - 40
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30047-8_2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043900759?origin=resultslist
AD  - Publicis Groupe, Prosper, 75078, TX, United States
AD  - Cigna Corp, Bloomfield, 06002, CT, United States
AB  - Enterprise workplace systems increasingly rely on derived or inferred attributes—such as risk tiers, propensity scores, or eligibility flags—to support operational decisions. These shadow inferences are often generated automatically and propagated across workflows, yet remain largely invisible and uncontrollable to the people affected by them. Existing privacy and transparency mechanisms focus primarily on consent at data collection or post-hoc explanations, offering limited support for managing the downstream use and spread of inferences in everyday work. This paper reframes privacy and trust in enterprise systems as a problem of actionable control over inferences, rather than consent over inputs. We introduce three reusable interface patterns that operationalize this shift within routine workflows: Inline Inference Badges, which surface the presence and confidence of inferred attributes at the point of use; an Inference Ledger, which provides provenance and visibility into how inferences are created and reused; and Quarantine Controls, which allow users to suppress or bound the propagation of specific inferences with auditable scope and duration. We evaluate these patterns through a mixed-methods study comprising a within-subjects experiment with 66 domain professionals performing realistic casework tasks across four interface conditions, complemented by an administrative audit study with 18 compliance stakeholders. Measures include decision quality, disclosure incidents, suppression effectiveness, user trust and workload, and audit accuracy. By making inferred attributes visible, governable, and accountable at the interaction level, this work advances human-centered approaches to responsible AI deployment in enterprise systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
