AD  - Ksr Institute for Engineering and Technology, Department of biomedical engineering, Tiruchengode, India
AB  - Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is a major and progressive complication frequently affecting individuals with diabetes, and its early recognition is crucial to prevent severe renal impairment. With the growth of biomedical data and modern computational tools, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has emerged as an important approach for improving kidney health assessment. This paper presents a structured review of AI techniques used for monitoring renal function in diabetic patients. The analysis covers conventional machine learning algorithms, advanced deep learning models, explainable AI approaches, and IoT-enabled monitoring systems by evaluating relevant studies from established scientific databases. The review shows that classical machine learning models remain valuable due to their transparency and lower computational demands, whereas deep learning techniques provide enhanced predictive capability by identifying complex patterns in physiological and biochemical data. IoT and cloud-based architectures support continuous, real-time data collection but still encounter challenges concerning sensor stability, data protection, and large-scale deployment. Major research gaps highlighted include limited diversity in datasets, lack of multimodal data integration, minimal external clinical validation, and insufficient adoption of interpretability methods. Overall, the findings emphasise the need for reliable, transparent, and clinically tested AI frameworks to improve early CKD detection and tailored management for diabetic patients. © 2026 IEEE.
