VL  - 17
IS  - 10
SP  - 1455
EP  - 1469
DO  - 10.14569/IJACSA.2026.01710125
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055722890?origin=resultslist
AD  - Mukesh Patel School of Technology Management and Engineering, Mumbai, India
AB  - Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) are widely used to support diagnosis, risk prediction, and treatment planning. However, many existing systems are limited by institution-centric data handling, weak auditability, and a lack of clear explanations at the point of clinical decision-making. Although blockchain-based federated learning approaches improve the security of model exchange, they often treat consent management, autonomous reasoning, model drift handling, and clinician-focused explainability as separate components. To address this gap, this study proposes CDSS-AgentXAI, a blockchainenhanced agentic AI framework for multi-institutional clinical decision support. The framework combines smart contract-based consent control, privacy-preserving federated learning, decisiontime explainable inference, and drift-aware model adaptation within one auditable pipeline. It specifies a permissioned Hyperledger Fabric layer for consent and audit tracking, FedProx aggregation with differentially private updates, and an agentic decision module that combines reinforcement learning with a neural network. Clinician-facing explanations are specified using SHAP, LIME, and counterfactual reasoning, and ADWIN-based drift monitoring is specified to trigger feedback-driven retraining. The federated learning and privacy layer was evaluated on 67,989 ICU stays from MIMIC-IV v3.1 across three simulated hospital nodes, with seven configurations compared over five random seeds. Test AUC-ROC ranged from 0.883 to 0.896, and FedProx with differential privacy (0.893 ± 0.003) did not differ significantly from the non-private and centralized models. © (2026), (Science and Information Organization). All rights reserved.
