TI  - Dynamic Digital Twins for trustworthy healthcare: Fusing multimodal EEG and endoscopic data for explainable clinical decision support
PY  - 2026
T2  - Array
VL  - 32
C7  - 101406
DO  - 10.1016/j.array.2026.101406
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055698853?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, India
AB  - Background: Translating data-driven AI innovations into clinically meaningful solutions requires addressing biomedical data complexity and the need for explainable models. We introduce a novel approach to personalized medicine by leveraging Dynamic Digital Twins (DDT) to represent real-time patient states. Methods: This study proposes an explainable, multimodal architecture combining 1D physiological signals (EEG arrays for cognitive state) and 2D medical imaging (wireless capsule endoscopy). Utilizing a Graph Attention Network (GAT) integrated with a federated learning framework, the system processes cross-modal features while preserving data privacy across distributed clinical nodes. Results: In rigorous clinical validation protocols, the multimodal DDT model achieved a diagnostic accuracy of 94.8% (95% CI: 93.1%–96.4%), representing a 14.2% improvement over unimodal baselines. Processing latency was reduced by 31%, and clinician-assessed trustworthiness improved significantly, with SHAP-based interpretability scores reaching 0.89/1.0. Conclusion: By successfully synthesizing cognitive biomarkers and localized imaging into a unified digital twin, this framework provides a highly accurate, transparent, and privacy-preserving tool ready for real-world clinical implementation. © 2026 The Author
