TI  - A Human-in-the-Loop and Explainable AI Framework for Decision Support in Battery Lifecycle Management Using Digital Battery Passports
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Conference on Human Centric Artificial Intelligence, ICHCAI 2026 - 1st International Conference
DO  - 10.1109/ICHCAI70183.2026.11607681
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046223638?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of Sri Jayewardenepura, Resource Economics Unit, Faculty of Applied Sciences, Department of Forestry and Environmental Science, Sri Lanka
AD  - Norwegian University of Life Sciences (NMBU), Department of Data Science, Norway
AB  - Lithium-ion batteries are central to electrified transport and renewable energy systems, yet evaluating their sustainability under real operating conditions remains challenging. Conventional life cycle assessment (LCA) approaches rely on static assumptions, generic datasets, and nominal performance values, limiting their ability to support timely and contextaware decision-making across battery use, second-life deployment, remanufacturing, and end-of-life management. These limitations are particularly critical in industrial settings where human actors must make safety-, sustainability-, and valuesensitive decisions under uncertainty. This paper proposes a human-centric Artificial Intelligence (AI) framework for dynamic LCA based on a Digital Battery Passport (DBP) architecture that integrates real-time operational data into environmental assessment and decision support. The system treats the passport as a continuously evolving digital entity that ingests telemetry from the Battery Management System (BMS), interfaces with enterprise systems, and updates environmental indicators through a Digital Twin (DT) environment. AI-enabled analytics interpret degradation behavior, estimate State of Health (SoH), detect anomalies, and generate explainable recommendations for human stakeholders such as fleet operators, sustainability managers, remanufacturing engineers, and recyclers. A dynamic functional unit based on cumulative delivered energy is introduced to replace static capacity-based metrics, enabling a more realistic representation of battery performance over time. The framework supports continuous recalculation of carbon footprint and related environmental indicators, while preserving human oversight through transparent indicators, role-based access, and auditable event logging. Use cases are demonstrated for firstlife operation, second-life classification, and end-of-life pathway selection. The results show that integrating DBP, DT infrastructure, and explainable AI establishes a scalable foundation for human-centered environmental intelligence in the circular battery economy. The challenges include trust, interpretability, data governance, interoperability, SME adoption barriers, and uncertainty in battery health estimation. The proposed approach positions dynamic LCA not only as an automation tool, but as a human-in-the-loop AI system for critical sustainability decisionmaking.  © 2026 IEEE.
