AD  - College of Engineering, Kallooppara, Kerala, India
AD  - Sri Buddha College of Engineering, Kerala, India
AB  - Clinical decision support systems increasingly rely on large language models and deep learning; however, existing approaches often suffer from hallucination, limited explainability, and systematic bias toward common diseases. These limitations are particularly critical in safety-sensitive medical settings, where unverifiable predictions and rare disease suppression can lead to severe clinical consequences. This paper presents CareFusion AI, an evidence-based, agentic, multi-modal clinical decision support framework designed to operate in a local-first and privacy-preserving environment. The system integrates four complementary intelligence modules: (i) symptom-based reasoning using closed-vocabulary extraction and hierarchical bucketed anomaly detection, (ii) explainable medical image analysis with enforced spatial validity, (iii) deterministic genomic variant interpretation grounded in ClinVar evidence, and (iv) temporal risk trajectory modeling using longitudinal patient data. To prevent unsupported inference, all generative reasoning is explicitly constrained by retrieved clinical evidence through a verification-oriented retrieval-augmented generation pipeline. The proposed architecture emphasizes transparency, auditability, and conservative decision-making rather than autonomous diagnosis. Experimental evaluation on public and anonymized datasets, including MIMIC-IV, NIH Chest X-ray, and ClinVar, demonstrates strong diagnostic coverage, low hallucination rates, and clinically meaningful explainability across modalities. These results indicate that CareFusion AI provides a robust foundation for safe, multi-modal clinical decision support in research and resource-constrained environments.  © 2026 IEEE.
