TI  - Beyond Performance Metrics: Nurses’ Concerns and Comfort with Clinical AI Use in Canada
PY  - 2027
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
VL  - 16748 LNCS
SP  - 54
EP  - 58
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30710-1_7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045851981?origin=resultslist
AD  - Telfer School of Management, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON, Canada
AB  - Nurses represent frontline users of Artificial Intelligence (AI) enabled decision support, monitoring, and workflow systems, but their perspectives remain underrepresented in AI implementation research. This study examines the nature and level of concerns affecting nurses’ comfort with AI use in Canada. Secondary data from the 2023 Canada Health Infoway nurses’ survey (N = 1,907) were analyzed to examine the association between self-reported comfort (dependent variable) and concerns with AI use (independent variables). Multivariate ordinal logistic regression analyses assessed the relationship between comfort with AI use and nine concern items (4-point Likert Scale, 1 = very uncomfortable or concerning to 4 = very comfortable or not at all concerning) covering three concern areas (i.e. ethical/legal; human-relationship; workforce), while controlling for age and gender. Most respondents were female (91.5%) and [35–54] years old (53.7%). Median concern levels ranged from 1 to 2, with highest concerns related to data privacy and reduced human interaction (median = 1, respectively). Comfort with AI use was moderate (median = 2). Lower comfort was significantly associated with older age (τ = −0.066) and female gender (χ2 (2) = 6.18). Multivariate analyses showed that greater concerns with medical liability (β = 0.363), negative patient-provider relationship impact (β = 0.409), and mistrust in AI (β = 0.498) are significantly associated with lower comfort. Nurses’ comfort with AI use is associated with AI trust and accountability concerns. Future research should build on these findings and investigate how AI knowledge may affect this relationship. Designing AI tools requires in addition to algorithmic transparency and performance validation, liability frameworks, explainability, and clinician-centered interaction models that alleviate users’ concerns. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
