DO  - 10.1109/ICIPTM69057.2026.11466009
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037825699?origin=resultslist
AD  - Amity University, Uttar Pradesh, Noida, India
AD  - Sgtb Khalsa College, University of Delhi, North Campus, New Delhi, India
AD  - University of Melbourne, Faculty of Science, Australia
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) is increasingly transforming traditional financial systems through data-driven, automated, and scalable decision-making processes. Techniques such as machine learning, predictive analytics, and natural language processing are being deployed to provide personalized recommendations and optimize investment strategies at a scale unattainable by human advisors. However, despite these advancements, adoption remains constrained by concerns over algorithmic transparency, data privacy, regulatory compliance, and user trust. To address these challenges, this study adopts an extended Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) model to empirically investigate the determinants of user adoption of AI-driven financial decision tools. A structured survey of 200 respondents-comprising both professionals and individual investors-was conducted to examine perceptions related to performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, facilitating conditions, and trust. The findings reveal that while performance expectancy (78%) and ease of use (85%) strongly drive adoption, a significant portion of users (62%) remain hesitant due to security and transparency concerns. Younger demographics (ages 25-35) exhibited higher acceptance rates, influenced by digital familiarity and gamified interfaces that enhance engagement and motivation. The research contributes both theoretically and practically by extending the UTAUT framework to integrate gamification as a behavioural enabler that strengthens trust, reduces cognitive resistance, and sustains user participation. The study concludes by proposing a multi-layered adoption strategy combining explainable AI (XAI), federated learning for privacy-preserving analytics, and gamification-based engagement mechanisms to foster transparency, confidence, and long-term user involvement in AIdriven wealth management. © 2026 IEEE.
