AD  - Department of Forensic Sciences, Sungkyunkwan University, Seoul, 03063, South Korea
AD  - Korean Police Investigation Academy, Asan-si, Chungcheongnam-do, 31540, South Korea
AB  - Digital forensic casework rarely proceeds under laboratory conditions. Under time and resource constraints, analysts often triage thousands of seized text files, with critical documents embedded in large volumes of irrelevant material. This study presents a lightweight triage approach for prioritizing clandestine operational instruction (COI) documents in CPU-only deployment environments. The method combines TF-IDF features, a LinearSVC classifier, a tunable decision threshold, and SHAP-based analyst-facing rationale. Cross-case transfer was evaluated with a leave-one-case-out (LOCO) protocol using 133 court-adjudicated COI documents from two independent investigations and 500 synthetic negatives constructed as a structured stress-test contrast set. Across the two folds, the model achieved a mean accuracy of 99.29% and a mean F1 of 98.28%, with 5 false negatives in total (fold-wise FNR: 2.27% and 4.49%; pooled FNR: 3.76%). End-to-end screening required 0.32 ms per document, with a 1.34 MB model footprint on a single-threaded CPU. Comparisons with transformer-based and lightweight baselines showed that this configuration provided a favorable balance of accuracy, latency, model size, and feature-level auditability under CPU-constrained conditions. A proxy false-positive evaluation on 9,606 Korean Wikipedia paragraphs from KorQuAD v1.0 yielded a false-positive rate of 0.31% (95% CI: 0.21%–0.45%). Work Saved over Sampling reached 79%. The system is intended as a screening aid within a tiered forensic workflow. SHAP outputs are designed to support analyst review rather than serve as standalone evidentiary instruments. The findings are limited to the evaluated Korean-language counterintelligence cases and have not been validated across other languages, crime types, or jurisdictions. © 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License.
