AB  - By utilizing cutting-edge algorithms and data analytics, machine learning (ML) has the potential to completely improve healthcare. This chapter examines the revolutionary impact of ML in disease detection and prediction. In order to improve patient outcomes and maximize healthcare resources, it starts by highlighting the need for fast and precise disease detection. The principles of ML are covered, including data preprocessing strategies necessary for managing intricate healthcare datasets as well as supervised, unsupervised, and semi-supervised learning approaches. The applicability of well-known algorithms for illness prediction and diagnostic tasks—including logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, support vector machines (SVMs), and neural networks—is examined. In addition, the chapter discusses the significance of feature selection, extraction, and dimensionality reduction in improving model performance. Evaluation metrics, such as accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, and area under the curve (AUC), are then thoroughly covered. There is a thorough analysis of ethical issues, including issues with interpretability, algorithmic bias, and data privacy, as well as methods for promoting equity and openness in prediction models. Future developments in disease detection and prediction are expected to be largely driven by emerging technologies like blockchain, the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT), and genomics. Forecasts for the development of ML in the healthcare industry highlight developments like explainable artificial intelligence (XAI), improved clinical decision support systems, and predictive analytics. In order to guarantee responsible innovation and fair healthcare delivery, the chapter ends with a call to action for more multidisciplinary research, ethical application, and the adoption of patient-centric approaches. This thorough investigation highlights how ML has the revolutionary potential to redefine illness diagnosis and prediction for better patient outcomes and increased healthcare efficiency. © 2025 CRC Press.
