AD  - Mohan Babu University [Erstwhile Sree Vidyanikethan Engineering College], Department Of Ece, Tirupati, India
AD  - Saveetha School Of Engineering, Simats, Department Of Chemistry, Tamilnadu, Chennai, India
AD  - Fractal Analytics, Bengaluru, India
AB  - The fast growth of Internet of Things (IoT) in healthcare has allowed real-time patient data monitoring, but issues of data privacy, explainability, and adaptive decision-making are not yet resolved. This paper proposes AFNS-IoT, an Adaptive Federated Neuro-Symbolic IoT framework that integrates deep learning, neuro-symbolic reasoning, and reinforcement learning for intelligent and explainable healthcare decision-making. Leveraging the MIMIC-IV dataset, local IoT edge nodes are trained using federated deep learning models to preserve patient privacy, while a multi-objective weighted aggregation balances accuracy, latency, and device constraints. A neuro-symbolic fusion layer translates model outputs into interpretable symbolic rules, providing transparent decision pathways for clinical decision support. Furthermore, a reinforcement learning feedback loop adaptively refines the system based on real-world outcomes. Experimental results demonstrate that AFNS-IoT achieves the highest accuracy (0.94), F1-score (0.92), and AUC (0.95), outperforming all baseline models. Furthermore, it reduces inference latency to 140 ms, compared to 220 ms in centralized learning, while achieving superior energy efficiency (0.88). Unlike conventional deep learning and federated approaches that lack interpretability, AFNS-IoT provides very high interpretability fidelity through its neuro-symbolic fusion layer. The findings demonstrate how AFNS-IoT can be used as the next-generation framework of privacy, explainable and adaptive healthcare IoT systems. © 2026 IEEE.
KW  - Deep Learning
KW  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence
KW  - Federated Learning
