TI  - Design of a Compliance-Driven Explainable AI Architecture for AML/CFT Decision Support in Financial Institutions
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 International Conference on Current Research in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, ICCRAIDS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICCRAIDS67816.2026.11519631
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041634421?origin=resultslist
AD  - Bina Nusantara University, Faculty of Engineering, Professional Engineer Program Department, Jakarta, 11480, Indonesia
AD  - Bina Nusantara University, Research Interest Group in Education Technology, Jakarta, 11480, Indonesia
AB  - Financial institutions face increasing regulatory pressure to enhance Anti-Money Laundering and CounterTerrorist Financing (AML/CFT) programs while ensuring transparency, auditability, and defensibility of automated decision-making. Many existing AML/CFT solutions rely on fragmented rule-based or machine learning approaches, resulting in limited explainability and weak decision traceability for regulatory review. This research proposes a compliancedriven explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) reference architecture for AML/CFT decision support, explicitly designed to align analytical capabilities with governance and supervisory expectations. The architecture integrates three complementary components: rule-based controls to encode regulatory and institutional obligations, machine learning-based anomaly detection to surface emerging risk patterns, and graph analytics to capture complex transactional and network relationships. Explainability and auditability are embedded as architectural design principles, enabling end-to-end decision traceability, model and rule lineage, and the generation of compliance evidence suitable for supervisory examination. Rather than introducing a new detection algorithm, this research contributes a reference architecture that operationalizes XAI in regulated AML/CFT environments. By harmonizing multiple analytical methods with compliance-by-design principles and legacy system interoperability, the proposed architecture addresses a critical gap between advanced analytics and regulatory expectations, supporting more transparent, governable, and defensible AML/CFT decision-making. © 2026 IEEE.
