AB  - Aging and related diseases account for ⁓70 % of global mortality with declining physiological homeostasis and cognitive function increasing susceptibility to neurodegenerative disorders, cardiovascular diseases and cancer. Treatment is hampered by imprecise diagnostics and limited therapeutic options. Precision medicine involving artificial intelligence (AI)-powered Aging Digital Twin (ADT) technology which integrates multi-source heterogeneous data via advanced AI and deep learning has great potential. Virtual doppelgangers enable real-time health monitoring, predictive analytics and personalized interventions to facilitate early disease detection and optimize risk assessment and treatment strategies. Challenges remain and include age-stratified biases in training datasets and variations in data quality for older populations which may limit model generalizability. Critical concerns regarding data privacy, algorithmic transparency and clinical validation of ADT models remain unresolved. The current review examines the transformative potential of AI-enabled ADTs in precision medicine for aging and highlights technical and ethical limitations. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
