AB  - As artificial intelligence (AI) transitions from experimental modeling to practical decision support in real estate, the field must clarify where research has accumulated and what remains uncertain. This study reviews the current state of AI in real estate literature by combining bibliometric mapping with a systematic literature review (SLR) of 127 peer-reviewed publications published up to 2025. The bibliometric analysis reveals a strong increase in research attention since 2018, with a focus on valuation, price modeling, and forecasting. It also identifies two key developments: (i) growing emphasis on interpretability and explainable AI, driven by the need for defensible valuation outputs; and (ii) the integration of AI within broader digitalization ecosystems, where geographic information systems (GIS), building information modeling (BIM), PropTech, FinTech, IoT, blockchain, and automated information extraction increasingly appear together. The SLR organizes evidence into four application domains: property valuation and appraisal; market analysis and forecasting; customer interaction; and property management. Consistent concerns emerge across these domains, including data quality and availability, limited model transparency, weak accountability outputs, and practical barriers related to organizational capabilities and governance. This review demonstrates that AI is becoming a central part of discussions about how decisions are made, justified, and implemented. However, the literature remains fragmented across methods, domains, and contexts. By clarifying dominant themes, underdeveloped areas, and implementation conditions, this study provides a structural basis for future research, stronger reporting norms, and more productive collaboration between academia and industry. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
