AD  - D. Serikbayev East Kazakhstan Technical University, School of Digital Technology, Ust-Kamenogorsk, Kazakhstan
AD  - D. Serikbayev East Kazakhstan Technical University, International School of Engineering, Ust-Kamenogorsk, Kazakhstan
AB  - The growing adoption of artificial intelligence in healthcare improves diagnostic accuracy and clinical decision support but also creates significant challenges in protecting sensitive patient data. This paper presents an AI-based clinical decision support portal for blood gas analysis and secure patient data management, designed for the diagnosis of diabetic and hematologic syndromes using arterial blood gas analysis. The portal is implemented as a modular full-stack platform based on ReactJS, Express.js, Sequelize ORM, and PostgreSQL, integrating both local and remote large language models for clinical text interpretation and diagnostic reasoning. The study focuses on secure system architecture and privacy-preserving design for AI-enabled healthcare applications. A comprehensive cybersecurity analysis addresses threats such as unauthorized access, API abuse, credential compromise, prompt injection, and risks of sensitive data leakage during external LLM interaction. To mitigate these risks, a multi-layered security framework is implemented, including JWT-based authentication, role-based access control, encrypted credential storage, rate limiting, secure deployment practices, and network isolation. A local anonymization module removes personally identifiable information before any interaction with external LLM services, significantly reducing privacy risks while preserving the effectiveness of AI-assisted diagnostics. The proposed architecture demonstrates a secure and scalable approach to integrating AI into clinical decision support systems and highlights the importance of combining explainable AI with strong cybersecurity mechanisms in modern digital healthcare. © 2026 IEEE.
