AB  - Grappling heterogeneous healthcare data and bringing with it a demand for advanced AI systems is also the need to integrate multi-modal information while comprising in the patient private requirements for interpretability. Flexible multi-modal explainable AI system for personalized healthcare decision support within a federated learning framework is what research is proposing. It improves diagnosis accuracy through an attention-based fusion mechanism that dynamically balances contributions from distributed data modalities of patients, such as genomics, wearable sensor data, clinical notes, and medical images. By not requiring sharing sensitive raw data, federated learning also minimizes ethical and legal liabilities which hinder cooperative training of models among health organizations. For building confidence and ease in human-AI collaboration, the integrated explainability module would provide modality-specific, clinically applicable explanations. Experimental results using synthetic federated datasets show substantial improvements in explanation fidelity, privacy, communication efficiency, and predictive performance when compared to baseline models. A scalable system is present for adapting to different clinical settings while becoming capable of tackling numerous large issues in privacy-aware interpretable AI in health- care. In this manner, it lays the groundwork for developing robust collaborative clinical practices of AI-assisted personalized medicine. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
