AB  - In oncology, artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly evolving from an auxiliary diagnostic tool to a core component of treatment decision-making and surgical practice. This narrative review evaluates the current applications of AI, challenges, and future developments in surgical oncology. We systematically review AI technologies, including precise image segmentation, three-dimensional reconstruction, virtual surgical planning, augmented reality-guided resection, intelligent surgical robotics, and multimodal prognosis prediction, across the surgical care continuum from preoperative planning to intraoperative navigation and postoperative management. We also critically discuss hurdles in clinical translation, including limited high-level evidence, high costs, system integration difficulties, data privacy concerns, algorithmic transparency, and ethical–regulatory dilemmas. We further consider the potential use of emerging technologies, such as multimodal data fusion, federated learning, explainable AI, and large language models, in surgical oncology. We anticipate that in the future, AI-augmented surgical oncology will be characterized by profound human–machine collaboration, with AI serving as a decision-support tool and a cognitive partner for surgeons. These advances will enhance the precision and reduce the invasiveness of cancer treatment while ensuring personalization and value-based care. Copyright © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Wolters Kluwer Health, LLC. on behalf of the Huazhong University of Science and Technology.
