AD  - CMR Institute of Technology, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Bengaluru, India
AB  - Lack of street lighting in the city is a major threat to the security of the population and the efficiency of the municipal machinery, but the proactive evaluation of infrastructure functioning is not yet developed. Although the shift towards carbon reduction using Light Emitting Diodes (LEDs) is growing, local governments often do not have a quantitative idea of how the factor of infrastructure parameters, including pole density, road length, and technology ratios, directly affect the adequacy of lighting. The study constructs a high-fidelity predictive method, based on supervised learning models, such as Random Forest (RF), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) and Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) networks, to project infrastructure input to service outcomes. The MLP model was found to have a better predictive accuracy using a detailed set of municipal ward data where Coefficient of Determination (R2) was 0.95 and root mean square error (RMSE) was significantly low. To achieve a transparency in the decision-making of the public sector, the study combined SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) to analyze the model and found pole density and LED penetration as the main factors that contribute to the lighting adequacy. These results offer an adequate and explainable approach to diagnosing infrastructure deficits and predicting the effects of technological improvements by the municipal planners. The research concludes that a shift towards qualitative audits to explainable predictive intelligence is the key to maximizing the urban light and effecting resource distribution.  © 2026 IEEE.
