AD  - Verizon, Irving, United States
AD  - Sap America Inc, Newtown Square, PA, United States
AB  - As AI systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains such as healthcare, finance, and criminal justice, the gap between a model's predictive capability and its explainability to diverse stakeholders constitutes an unmanaged organizational risk. Drawing on the established software engineering concept of technical debt, this paper introduces Explainability Debt (ED) as a measurable, actionable construct for responsible AI deployment. We formalize ED through four quantitative metrics: Explanation Coverage (EC), Fidelity Drift Index (FDI), Stakeholder Reach Score (SRS), and Auditability Completeness (AC). Together, these form the EFSA framework. We validate EFSA through case studies on three production-scale models deployed in clinical decision support and credit risk assessment, demonstrating that ED scores correlate significantly with downstream audit failures (r=0.81, p<0.001) and regulatory non-compliance incidents (r=0.74, p<0.01). Inter-rater reliability for human-scored components achieves Cohen's kappa=0.83. The EFSA framework provides practitioners with a concrete, repeatable instrument for measuring and managing explainability as a first-class engineering concern, directly supporting compliance with emerging AI governance requirements including the EU AI Act and NIST AI RMF.  © 2026 IEEE.
