AD  - Mohan Babu University, School Of Computing, Dept. Of Cse, A.P, Tirupati, India
AB  - Gynaecological disease diagnosis in resourceconstrained and linguistically diverse areas is still a difficult issue because of limited clinical access, privacy matters, and unchanging decision support systems. Current expert systems are based on centralised learning and superficial language processing, which are limiting scalability, flexibility and reliability. To overcome these challenges, this research would introduce a Federated and Incremental Multilingual Gynaecological Disease Diagnosis Expert System (FI-GDDES) that combines privacy-preserving federated learning, continual model adaptation, and explainable artificial intelligence. The suggested framework allows distributed healthcare nodes to jointly and continuously train diagnostic models without sharing raw patient information, while also accommodating new diseases, symptoms, and regional languages. A voice-based natural language understanding component identifies symptom representations in context, thereby strengthening the diagnosis. Substantial experiments carried out on multi-disease gynaecological data reveal that FI-GDDES is more accurate (97.2), more precise (96.8), higher in recall (97.5), and higher in F1-score (97.1) than centralised baselines (by as much as 4.1 points). The federated paradigm stabilises at a set of limited communication rounds. It maintains data privacy and interpretability and, as such, applies to real-world healthcare applications. © 2026 IEEE.
