AD  - R.V. College of Engineering, Bengaluru, India
AD  - Nvidia Corporation, TX, United States
AB  - The critical decision-making fields including health-care and finance and safety-critical systems require Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) to establish transparent systems which create trusting relationships between users and artificial intelligence systems while providing accountability. The actual process of selecting explainability techniques results in organizations using a single solution which fails to consider their specific needs for different stakeholder functions and project timelines and potential decision outcomes and their legal obligations. The process of choosing explanations through ad hoc methods will create untrustworthy and ineffective explanations which do not meet required standards. A new decision-centered framework for understanding risks explains this paper through its development of an explainability system which needs contextual details to determine which XAI methods should be verified. The proposed framework establishes a structured framework which organizations can use to connect their explanation methods with the specific decision-making circumstances. The researchers used the framework to study decision-making scenarios from medical imaging and credit scoring which showed the framework provides better outcomes in terms of latency restrictions and explanation consistency and user experience and regulatory compliance when compared to basic methods of choosing explanations. The study presents an explainability sufficiency checklist together with a regulatory compliance mapping tool which enables organizations to implement XAI systems in critical decision systems while ensuring they can undergo proper audits. © 2026 IEEE.
