TI  - SmartForest: Optimizing anemia diagnosis with enhanced random forest and XGBoost integration
PY  - 2025
T2  - Results in Engineering
VL  - 27
C7  - 106442
DO  - 10.1016/j.rineng.2025.106442
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105012534028?origin=resultslist
AD  - Informatics Study Program, Telkom University, Surabaya Campus, East Java, Surabaya, 60231, Indonesia
AD  - Center of Excellence for Motion Technology for Safety Health and Wellness, Research Institute of Sustainable Society, Telkom University, Surabaya Campus, East Java, Surabaya, 60231, Indonesia
AD  - Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Khon Kaen University, Khon Kaen, 40002, Thailand
AD  - Department of Clinical Pathology and Laboratory Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Public Health and Nursing, Gadjah Mada University, Yogyakarta, 55281, Indonesia
AD  - Department of Electrical Engineering, Faculty of Industrial Technology, Universitas Ahmad Dahlan, Yogyakarta, 55191, Indonesia
AD  - Department of Electrical Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Universitas Muhammadiyah Yogyakarta, Yogyakarta, 55183, Indonesia
AB  - This study addresses the challenges in healthcare classification, focusing specifically on the diagnosis of anemia. It introduces SmartForest, a novel ensemble model that integrates Random Forest, XGBoost, and sea horse optimization techniques to enhance both accuracy and interpretability. By incorporating explainable artificial intelligence (AI) through SHAP analysis, the model aims to support transparency and trust in clinical decision-making, thereby contributing to improved patient outcomes. SmartForest leverages a soft voting mechanism to aggregate the probability estimates from both ensemble learners, ensuring balanced and interpretable predictions. The sea horse optimization algorithm is employed to determine the optimal number and depth of trees, facilitating robust performance across diverse medical datasets. Experimental results demonstrate that SmartForest outperforms traditional models across multiple evaluation metrics. On the anemia dataset, it achieves an accuracy of 0.9882, precision of 0.9809, recall of 0.9939, and F1-score of 0.9872. The model also exhibits strong discriminatory power with a ROC-AUC of 0.9982, and produces minimal prediction error, reflected by a low MSE of 0.0118 and RMSE of 0.0799. While the average prediction time is slightly higher at 0.2501 seconds, this is a reasonable trade-off given the model's superior performance. The objective of this work was to improve anemia diagnosis using machine learning models, with SmartForest demonstrating the most promising results. Future research directions include expanding the dataset to include more diverse patient populations, integrating the model into real-world clinical decision support systems, enhancing model explainability, and exploring its applicability to other medical conditions. Ethical considerations surrounding data privacy and clinical deployment should also be addressed in future studies. © 2025 The Author(s)
