TI  - Adaptive and secure multimodal federated learning for distributed cardiovascular risk stratification with interpretable clinical decision support
PY  - 2026
T2  - Cluster Computing
VL  - 29
IS  - 11
C7  - 688
DO  - 10.1007/s10586-026-06421-1
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048066755?origin=resultslist
AD  - Shandong Xiehe University, Shandong, Jinan, 250109, China
AD  - Department of Engineering, Payame Noor University (PNU), Tehran, Iran
AB  - The use of machine learning models for cardiovascular risk assessment in decentralized healthcare settings is hindered by data silos, privacy constraints, multimodal heterogeneity, and restricted interpretability. Federated learning facilitates collaborative model training without the centralization of raw data; nevertheless, current methodologies frequently depend on static aggregation techniques, inadequate preprocessing frameworks, and insufficient empirical validation across diverse modalities. This paper presents an adaptable and secure multimodal federated learning system intended for distributed cardiovascular risk prediction inside clustered healthcare infrastructures. The proposed architecture includes: (i) modality-aware feature extraction for structured electronic health records, temporal wearable sensor signals, and cardiac imaging representations; (ii) a heterogeneity-sensitive preprocessing pipeline comprising statistical normalization, denoising-based feature refinement, and class-imbalance mitigation via synthetic minority augmentation; and (iii) an adaptive aggregation mechanism that dynamically re-weights client updates according to data distribution divergence and local validation stability. The methodology improves clinical transparency by incorporating post-hoc interpretability using Shapley-based feature attribution, facilitating both global and individualized risk explanations. Comprehensive experimental assessments are performed on both real-world and benchmark cardiovascular datasets. Comparative analyses encompass centralized learning, traditional FedAvg, various convolutional architectures, and alternative optimization methods to evaluate convergence behavior and computing efficiency. Ablation research measures the impact of each architectural element. The results indicate persistent enhancements in AUC, F1-score, Matthews Correlation Coefficient, balanced accuracy, and convergence stability in the presence of class imbalance. An analysis of computational overhead and communication costs is conducted to evaluate the viability of implementation in clustered medical systems. Privacy considerations are analyzed through the exploration of potential federated vulnerabilities and corresponding mitigation techniques. The results indicate that adaptive multimodal federated modeling, along with systematic preprocessing and interpretable decision support, offers a scalable and therapeutically feasible approach for distributed cardiovascular risk stratification. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
