AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) is revolutionizing psychiatry by shifting clinical practice from subjective judgments to data-driven, evidence-based approaches. Traditional methods like interviews and self-reports face limitations in consistency and scalability. Leveraging multimodal data-neuroimaging, EEG, speech, digital footprints, and health records-AI enables early detection, accurate diagnosis, and personalized treatment planning. Machine Learning models such as support vector machines and random forests handle structured data, while Deep Learning architectures like CNNs, RNNs, and transformers process complex multimodal inputs for enhanced prediction. Integrated into Clinical Decision Support Systems, AI assists in risk assessment, relapse monitoring, and individualized interventions. Yet challenges persist, including data scarcity, bias, privacy risks, and workflow integration. Addressing these demands explainable AI (XAI), fairness audits, federated learning, and culturally adaptive datasets. This study proposes a conceptual framework spanning multimodal data acquisition, interpretability, ethical governance, and clinical decision-making. Future progress lies in multimodal fusion, preventive and precision psychiatry, and transparent human-AI collaboration, positioning AI as a trustworthy ally in precision mental healthcare. © 2026 The Authors.
