AB  - Traditional 'black box' models lack transparency and explainability, making it difficult to meet the requirements of accountability and trust in high-risk decision making scenarios. This study proposes a method based on explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) to solve the problem of interpreting individual behavior evaluation results in black box models. The method integrates techniques such as Local Interpretable Model-Independent Explanations (LIME), SHapley Additive Explanations (SHAP), and interpretable decision trees to model and optimize behavior evaluation models. The specific methods include: (1) using deep neural networks to predict behavior data; (2) using LIME and SHAP to perform local and global feature importance analysis respectively to reveal the decision-making process of the model; (3) using interpretable decision trees to visualize the rules of key subgroups to enhance decision transparency. The optimized XAI system achieved an accuracy of approximately 84% on a public behavior evaluation dataset and achieved a maximum feature explanation consistency index of 0.94, effectively improving the model's interpretability and user trust. © 2025 IEEE.
