AB  - Rapid advances in machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) have transformed patient care, prediction, and image-based diagnosis. However, the black-box nature of many AI models raises concerns about trust, ethics, and accountability, limiting their clinical adoption. Explainable AI (XAI) addresses these challenges by promoting fairness, transparency, and alignment with medical reasoning. This chapter explores the use of XAI in medical imaging, electronic health records, and treatment planning. It reviews core explainability methods, model-agnostic techniques, feature-attribution approaches, and inherently interpretable models, highlighting how they improve diagnostic accuracy, build clinician trust, and support transparent decision-making. Widely used XAI tools like Grad-CAM, SHAP, LIME, and layer-wise relevance propagation are discussed in the context of regulatory compliance and clinical validation. Despite its promise, XAI faces limitations, including trade-offs between complexity and interpretability, potential biases in explanations, and the lack of standardized evaluation metrics. Hybrid approaches that combine clinical expertise with AI insights are emerging to offer responsible and transparent decision support. Integrating XAI into healthcare systems strengthens clinician–patient trust and supports the ethical deployment of AI technologies. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Ramkumar Rajendran, Soni Sweta, Divya Rohatgi, Supriya Khaitan, Hari Mohan Rai and Khursheed Aurangzeb; individual chapters, the contributors.
