AB  - Cardiovascular disease is one of the worldwide societal issues in health that requires timely and accurate risk stratification to implement effective intervention measures. The traditional prognostic tools (like the Framingham Risk Score) do not have the capabilities of reflecting non-linear interactions of clinical and lifestyle factors. The following paper submits AI techniques to massive amounts of biomedical data, which include clinical records, electronic health records, and publicly available repositories, in order to generate better results in the field of CVD risk. Data cleansing and feature engineering and normalization were part of our preprocessing pipeline, which was followed by training and validating models with Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, Deep Neural Networks. XGBoost appeared to have the best performance, with an AUC -ROC of 0.93 and outperforming the traditional models. SHAP-based estimates of feature of importance validated the clinical nature of age, systolic blood pressure and cholesterol and hence support its prognostic importance. Industrialization Techniques that explain AI contributed to enhanced interpretability and hence clinical usage. In general, we can find that AI-powered models significantly enhance predictive risk modeling and thus allow personalized cardiovascular care and preventive ones, but data privacy and ethics in the practice of the cardiovascular process are of primary importance. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
