AB  - GenAI and Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) are quickly transforming healthcare by facilitating the creation of massive volumes of data and clinical decision support and patient-centered applications. This survey provides a high-level overview of recent developments in generative models, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), diffusion models, and Large Language Models (LLMs), as applied to medical imaging, drug discovery, electronic health records, and personalized treatment. We further analyze the complementary value of XAI in increasing the interpretability, regulatory compliance, and trustworthiness of AI-driven systems. In the literature, GenAI shows promises for shortening pharmaceutical pipelines, augmenting imaging datasets, simulating clinical trials, and facilitating patient interaction with healthcare-targeted LLMs. Nevertheless, much work remains to be done to ensure data privacy, reduce hallucinations, address bias, and justify models in clinical practice. This survey identifies existing opportunities, key constraints, and future opportunities for multimodal generative AI and explainable frameworks in healthcare. We summarize that safe, trustworthy, and transformative adoption of AI in healthcare is achievable through the further development of clinical validation, the development of sustainable interpretability standards, and alignment with regulatory frameworks.  © 2026 IEEE.
