AB  - Emotion detection has emerged as a critical capability in artificial intelligence–driven systems, particularly in applications requiring human-centric interaction and decision support. While recent advances in machine learning and deep learning have significantly improved recognition accuracy from facial images, videos, and multimodal data, the opaque nature of these models limits their trustworthiness and real-world adoption. This paper presents a comprehensive review of AI-based emotion detection techniques with a primary focus on Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) as a core analytical dimension. Existing approaches are systematically examined across feature extraction strategies, learning architectures, dataset characteristics, and application contexts, highlighting how explainability mechanisms are integrated to enhance transparency and interpretability. The review critically analyzes recent developments in image- and video-based emotion recognition, identifies limitations related to generalization, multi-label affect modeling, and real-time deployment, and examines ethical challenges such as bias and accountability. By synthesizing current research through an explainability-centered framework, this study outlines key research gaps and future directions toward developing reliable, interpretable, and ethically aligned emotion detection systems. © 2026 The Author(s).
