TI  - Towards an Adaptive and Intelligent Dashboard for AI-Driven Clinical Decision Support
PY  - 2026
T2  - International IEEE Conference  proceedings, IS
IS  - 2026
DO  - 10.1109/IS71416.2026.11727218
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055700189?origin=resultslist
AD  - Dalhousie University, Faculty of Computer Science, Halifax, NS, Canada
AB  - Clinical AI dashboards can support risk prediction, patient prioritization, and clinicians' decision-making. However, moving such systems from prototype to clinical use requires more than accurate predictions. The dashboards should support adaptive configuration, guideline governance, confidence-aware recommendation delivery, privacy control, and human-in-the-loop feedback. This paper presents an adaptive architecture for a hybrid machine learning and large language model clinical AI dashboard for hypoxemia and hypertension risk prediction. Using a design-science approach, we translated clinicians' concerns and deployment requirements into adaptive intelligent-system features and operationalized them through a three-layer architecture: a predictive intelligence layer, an explanation and recommendation layer, and an adaptive control layer. The adaptive control layer governs how AI recommendations are configured, displayed, justified, filtered, and refined through clinician interaction. We instantiate the architecture through a dashboard prototype and a feedback-to-requirement mapping that shows how clinicians' concerns were translated into system mechanisms for guideline-source configuration, explanation personalization, confidence-threshold adjustment, privacy and security control, and structured accept/modify/override feedback. This work contributes a clinician-informed adaptive architecture for AI clinical decision support and demonstrates how dashboard-level intelligence can support localized guideline alignment, transparent recommendation delivery, clinician oversight, and continuous system refinement for safer clinical AI adoption. © 2026 IEEE.
