UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047322779?origin=resultslist
AD  - Faculty of Geoengineering, Institute of Spatial Management and Geography, University of Warmia and Mazury in Olsztyn, Prawocheńskiego 15, 10-720, Olsztyn, Poland
AD  - Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Warmia and Mazury in Olsztyn, Słoneczna 54, 10-710, Olsztyn, Poland
AB  - Decision support systems based on large language models (LLMs) are evolving rapidly, and one important design direction is to complement them with stable and interpretable machine learning components, such as rule-based systems. In this work, we present a decision support system framework that combines semantic clustering based on LLM embeddings and zero-shot semantic segmentation with explainable decision rules induced by the Learn From Examples by Modules 2 (LEM2) algorithm and embedded in an SQL database. An additional contribution of this work is a dedicated mechanism for embedding LEM2 rules in a Structured Query Language (SQL) environment, combined with a query formulation that enables classification to be performed as a single SQL query per object. The proposed framework enables the transformation of unstructured textual survey data into a discrete and understandable set of rules that can be directly used in database-driven decision support systems. The framework has potential applications in LLM-assisted user interaction systems and can be adapted to other survey-based decision problems, including citizen feedback systems and similar public-sector decision-support scenarios. The case study is based on unpublished survey data concerning the level of acceptance of proposed changes to property tax. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
