AB  - The rapid digitalization of smart grids has led to an exponential increase in heterogeneous data, encompassing real-time sensor streams, advanced metering infrastructure, and high-resolution visual inspection records. Efficient management and intelligent utilization of this data are critical to ensuring grid reliability, operational security, and long-term sustainability. Recent advances in pattern recognition, computer vision, and machine learning have introduced powerful tools to address the challenges associated with big data in modern power systems. This paper provides a comprehensive review of the integration of these techniques for smart grid data management. First, the structural architecture and data landscape of smart grids are outlined, establishing the foundation for advanced analytics. Next, the principles of pattern recognition and computer vision are examined, emphasizing their role as enabling technologies. Special attention is given to machine learning methods - including clustering, neural networks, and deep learning - that support anomaly detection, predictive maintenance, and load forecasting. Furthermore, applications of computer vision in automated visual inspections, UAV-based monitoring, and security surveillance are discussed, demonstrating the growing synergy between visual analytics and intelligent learning models. Finally, critical challenges such as data privacy, computational scalability, and real-time processing constraints are analyzed, along with emerging opportunities arising from edge computing, explainable AI, and hybrid ML-CV frameworks. The review concludes that the convergence of pattern recognition, computer vision, and machine learning is essential for enhancing smart grid resilience, sustainability, and autonomous decision-making capabilities.  © 2025 IEEE.
