AB  - The increase in the worldwide burden of mood disorders, including depression, presents a substantial clinical conundrum, with an estimated prevalence of 970 million affected individuals in the world. Although conventional diagnostic approaches are fairly reliable, being largely based on self-reporting and in-person evaluation, the respective nature has arguably impeded rapid early diagnosis or intervention. As a countermeasure, recent developments in AI, particularly in the field of Natural Language Processing, have offered novel prospects for detecting emotional and psychological distress through the analysis of linguistic and behavioural cues in digital realms. In this paper, we provide a literature review of AI-based sentiment analysis techniques that aim to identify indicators of mood dysregulation. The paper organizes, in a structured manner, the existing literature according to data modalities, model architectures, and contributions from lexical, syntactic, and affective language features. Publicly available datasets, including DAIC-WOZ, eRisk, and CLPsych, are assessed in terms of their clinical relevance and representativeness. In addition, the authors assesses important ethical considerations, such as algorithmic bias, data privacy, and risks of misclassification. By placing computational methodologies in the framework of psychological theory, this work aims toward the design of clinically meaningful, ethically rational, and interpretably sound AI systems for the detection and monitoring of mood disorders.  © 2025 IEEE.
