TI  - Illuminating Predictions: The Use and Evaluation of Explainable AI in Predictive Process Monitoring - A Systematic Review
PY  - 2025
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 270
SP  - 1149
EP  - 1158
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2025.09.236
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105024071542?origin=resultslist
AD  - Technical University of Munich, Boltzmannstraße 3, Garching near Munich, 85748, Germany
AB  - Predictive Process Monitoring (PPM) enables organizations to anticipate and manage future business events using machine learning models. However, the widespread adoption of PPM is hindered by the lack of transparency in these models, often referred to as the "black-box problem." Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) addresses this challenge by providing interpretability techniques that enhance trust, accountability, and regulatory compliance. This systematic review explores the state-of-the-art XAI methods applied in PPM, focusing on their integration with AI models, the types of PPM tasks they support, and the evaluation approaches used to assess explainability. The findings reveal a strong preference for post-hoc explainability methods, particularly SHAP-based feature importance explanations, with a growing interest in graph-based and visualization techniques. Additionally, while evaluating AI models is standard practice, systematic evaluation of explainers remains underdeveloped, with no dominant metric emerging. This review highlights key research gaps and suggests future directions for improving explainability evaluation and integrating intrinsic explainability into PPM systems. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.. All rights reserved.
