AB  - This research paper discusses how the objectives of Islamic law can be used as a practical framework for designing modern artificial intelligence systems and controlling them by activating ideal values in the development of such software. These ethics and ideal values are essential objectives that contribute to the preservation of humanity. This study addresses three real-world areas in which AI ethics are of utmost importance: first, medical diagnosis; second, automated resume-based hiring; and third, deepfakes. These three examples reveal common ethical concerns that arise through practical application, not just theory. Based on these findings, this paper presents the MAAG (Maqasid-Aligned AI Governance) framework, which promotes the five fundamental principles of Islamic law: protection of life, mind, dignity, moral values, and property. The foundation and mechanisms are verifiable controls for artificial intelligence systems. The basic assumption is that the objectives of Islamic law are the controls and foundations through which the greatest gains are achieved. Future research could develop assessment tools to measure ethical risk levels across different sectors and technologies. © 2025 IEEE.
