TI  - Hacking the Black Box: Prompt Injection for Visualizing LLM Reasoning as Graphs
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - IIAI-AAI-Winter 2025 - 2025 19th IIAI International Congress on Advanced Applied Informatics
SP  - 303
EP  - 306
DO  - 10.1109/IIAI-AAI-Winter69777.2025.00064
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036211505?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computer Science, Tokyo University of Technology, Tokyo, Hachioji, Japan
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) show impressive versatility in translation, summarization, dialogue generation, and reasoning; however, their internal inference mechanisms remain opaque. This lack of transparency limits trust and poses challenges in education, healthcare, and decision support, where interpretability and accountability are essential features. To address this, we propose a lightweight framework that uses prompt injection to induce LLMs to explicitly output their reasoning traces. These traces, which contain assumptions, intermediate steps, and dependencies, are automatically parsed and visualized as directed graphs, enabling users to inspect the reasoning process step-by-step. Unlike traditional XAI methods, such as LIME or SHAP, which focus on feature attribution, our approach captures the sequential reasoning flow characteristic of generative models. By generating multiple traces for the same input and comparing their structural similarities, the framework also provides a quantitative measure of reasoning stability and reproducibility. Experiments with the Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite model showed that the method effectively externalizes reasoning paths and maintains task focus, even when prompts include distracting content. Although self-reported traces may not fully reflect internal computations, the proposed framework offers a practical and interpretable window into model behavior. It supports visual auditing, transparency analysis, and educational applications, contributing to a deeper alignment between human reasoning and machine cognition. ©2025 IEEE.
