AB  - Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become a life-changing contributor to contemporary computing systems, making machines capable of producing human-like outputs in various domains, including text, image, music and code. Although these capabilities open up new vistas of automation and creativity, they also bring a spectrum of ethical issues, durability and interoperability in machines; data privacy breaches; algorithmic biases; concerns about content authenticity and the possibility for misuse. In this paper, we explore the ethical aspect of Generative AI and provide a responsible governance-based approach to achieve a balance between openness and accountability. Building on a review of recent literature and on empirical evidence from real-world AI deployments, we investigate how ethical lapses may become visible in generative models and point to the limitations in existing regulation and design practices. We present an end-to-end framework that systematically embeds transparency, fairness, and accountability in the AI lifecycle by applying bias mitigation methods, explainable AI modules, and risk-aware optimization techniques. The key part of our approach is to tradeoff between content fluency and ethical considerations on real datasets. Experimental results show that our method can maintain the performance and reduce the population of ethical violations. The findings suggest that incorporating ethical reasoning into Generative AI models does not come at the cost of stifled innovation. The paper ends by presenting institutional-level recommendations to utilize Generative AI with a sense of responsibility across the university, industry and governance.  © 2025 IEEE.
