AB  - The integration of robotics into humanitarian operations, encompassing search and rescue, disaster response, and aid delivery, presents a profound opportunity to mitigate human risk and enhance operational efficacy. However, the deployment of autonomous systems in such high-stakes, ethically sensitive environments necessitates decision-making processes that are not only robust but also transparent and accountable. Opaque "black-box"artificial intelligence models pose a significant barrier to trust and adoption, as they preclude critical oversight from human operators and stakeholders. This paper argues for the critical integration of Explainable AI (XAI) methodologies as a foundational component of ethical decision support systems in humanitarian robotics. We posit that XAI can bridge the trust deficit by providing interpretable rationales for autonomous decisions, thereby enabling human-in-the-loop oversight, facilitating post-hoc accountability, and ensuring that robotic actions align with established humanitarian principles and international law. By examining current XAI paradigms - including model-agnostic techniques, inherently interpretable models, and human-centric explanation interfaces - this research delineates a framework for embedding ethical reasoning into the autonomy stack. The ultimate objective is to foster the development of humanitarian robots that are not only operationally capable but also morally competent and trustworthy partners in safeguarding human dignity during crises. © 2025 IEEE.
