TI  - A Two-Stage Framework for Explainable Clustering in Public-Sector Analytics: Algorithm Selection and Post-Hoc Validation
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Big Data, BigData
IS  - 2025
SP  - 2627
EP  - 2634
DO  - 10.1109/BigData66926.2025.11400778
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037864594?origin=resultslist
AD  - Escola de Administração de Empresas de São Paulo - EAESP, Fundação Getúlio Vargas - FGV, São Paulo, Brazil
AB  - We present and validate a two-stage framework for explainable clustering of public-sector units that balances technical validity and managerial adoption. Stage 1 formalizes algorithm selection as a transparent multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) problem that combines internal indices (Silhouette, Calinski-Harabasz, Davies-Bouldin) with stability (Adjusted Rand Index), scalability (median runtime), and interpretability. Stage 2 withholds a business outcome and conducts post-hoc validation via ANOVA/Welch and Tukey/Games-Howell. On 59,444 unit-month records from a large public financial institution, the framework selects K-means (k=7) and identifies seven organizational archetypes with statistically significant differences in financial performance. We report 20 independent runs per configuration, fixed seeds, and reproducible settings. The archetypes support targeted HR interventions and evidence-based management, empirically reinforcing Contingency Theory (fit between workforce and territorial context). The study illustrates how explainable clustering can translate large-scale administrative data into trustworthy, auditable decision support for government applications. © 2025 IEEE.
