TI  - Artificial Intelligence in MOOC Analytics: A Critical Review of Prediction Methods and Future Directions
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 World Skills Conference on Universal Data Analytics and Sciences, WorldSUAS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/WorldSUAS66815.2025.11199106
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022296155?origin=resultslist
AD  - SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Networking and Communications, Tamil Nadu, Kattankulathur, 603203, India
AB  - Massive Open Online Courses (MOOCs) have become pivotal in democratizing education, offering flexible learning opportunities to diverse global audiences. With increasing learner data volumes, Artificial Intelligence (AI) is being actively integrated into MOOC platforms to support informed decision-making and personalized learning. This survey critically examines recent AI-driven approaches for predictive analytics in MOOC environments. Despite these advancements, MOOCs still grapple with issues such as high learner dropout rates, insufficient engagement modeling, limited personalization, and lack of transparency in automated decisions. Moreover, challenges in handling imbalanced data, preserving user privacy, and building generalizable models across heterogeneous learner populations persist. To address these challenges, we present an AI-powered MOOC analytics architecture that incorporates data collection, preprocessing, machine learning and deep learning models, and modules for dropout prediction, engagement forecasting, and adaptive recommendation. The architecture emphasizes ensemble and hybrid models, interpretable predictions, privacy-aware computation (e.g., federated learning), and actionable visual dashboards for educators. Evaluation strategies using metrics like ROC-AUC and F1-score are also reviewed to assess performance robustness. This survey highlights the research gaps and proposes future directions for developing ethical, explainable, and scalable AI systems tailored for online learning environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
