AD  - Rajiv Gandhi Prodyogiki Vishwavidhalaya, Bhopal, India
AB  - The incorporation of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) methodologies has several novel opportunities such as earlier tumor detection, better surgical planning, and personalized care. The combination of new methods in the advanced imaging pipeline (pre-processing, segmentation, classification, and decision support) shows that convolutional, hybrid, and ensemble models have the ability to achieve more than 90% accuracy on public MRI datasets. In some cases, AI models achieve close to 100% accuracy on small datasets through transfer learning and image fusion-based features. According to the review, AI application in neuro-oncological imaging delays diagnosis and thereby increases specialist availability and enables cost-effective triage, especially in underdeveloped areas. For effective AI benefits safe swallow, we emphasize certain prerequisites for clinical implementation, which include: privacy-preserving multi-center data sharing, an explainable AI to reveal solutions for the black-box opacity, strict external validation on heterogeneous populations and different scanners, and workforce/infrastructure preparedness (e.g., federated deployment, optimized protocol, and user training). The review finishes with recommendations and research gaps designed to address the challenges experienced with the implementation studies to provide fair, dependable, and wide-reaching care in neuro-oncology. © 2025 IEEE.
