N1  - Funding text 1: Machine learning (ML) is a powerful tool for analyzing large, complex data and inferring relationships between the feature variables and the target outcome. The use of ML techniques in healthcare has become increasingly popular [12]. It is widely used in medical diagnosis, and prognosis as well as in the analysis of large electronic health records (EHR) and medical images [ 13\u201315]. ML algorithms have been successfully applied in developing decision-support systems for clinicians to assist in the critical decision-making process [16,17]. The use of such approaches for the detection of OC can be a promising step forward in the battle against this disease. ML can be extremely useful in detecting cancer in its earliest and most treatable stages, thereby increasing the chances of survival. It can efficiently analyze the patient's data and deduce relationships between patients' characteristics and conditions. On that account, a dataset containing the records of 171 OC patients and 178 patients with benign tumors is utilized in this study to develop an ML model that can accurately distinguish malignant tumors from benign ones. The data was originally collected from the Third Affiliated Hospital of Soochow University from 2011 to 2018 [18]. The goal is to develop a reliable decision-support system with the most appropriate biomarkers to help clinicians predict the presence of OC in patients using ML.
